From 2ffdf8fa63fb82affae889a0bf535e6dcacf3a74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Mohamed R. Khalil" <m.radwan.khalil@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:51:40 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] fix(v2): keep per-folder plugin setup off the shared event
 loop

Every new OpenChamber chat folder runs the full plugin setup on the backend's one
event loop. A CPU profile of that setup (isolated sandbox, the owner's real config:
25 agents, 54 commands, 187 skill files) showed the synchronous work that stalls
every other session while a folder activates:

- detectWorktreePath (three copies: skills loader, command discovery, compat
  command loader) ran `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` with execFileSync for each
  new folder, even when no ancestor has a .git marker (chat folders never do), so
  git can only fail; timeout 5 s each. Skip the spawn when there is no marker and
  no GIT_DIR.
- resolveSkillPathReferences used a lookbehind regex that JSC evaluates at every
  offset: about 45 ms per pass over the 2.4 MB of skill text, once per folder.
  Scan for "@" and check the preceding character by hand: identical output
  (verified on all 187 files), about 0.4 ms.
- team-mode dependency probes (tmux, git) spawned two processes per folder; probe
  once per process with the default spawner.
- built-in MCP runtime lookups ran a synchronous PATH search per folder; remember
  default lookups per PATH value.
- yield to the event loop between setup phases so one folder never holds it for
  the whole setup.

Isolated setup per new folder (steady state, 6 runs): 600-670 ms -> about 220 ms;
time the loop is blocked 190-250 ms -> about 55 ms; longest synchronous slice
about 200 ms -> about 55 ms (the remaining slice is fix 28's source fingerprint
walk). No git process is spawned for a chat folder any more.

(cherry picked from commit b2dc5e08b79c7553a4eb883767ffe804f1fbaea3)

Review R5: the team-mode probes are shared per process but bounded. Every probe
has a 3 s deadline and is killed on timeout, so callers always settle; concurrent
callers share one in-flight probe; "available" is remembered per PATH value; a
definite "unavailable" is remembered for 5 minutes and then retried; a timeout
is never remembered. Injected spawners bypass the shared cache but keep the
deadline.
